\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.3}{\ignorespaces The light blue and light red lines show the design sensitivity of the aLIGO and LIGO A+ detectors. The noise due to radiation pressure and photon shot noise is shown in dark blue (aLIGO) and dark red (A+). These two noise sources make up the detectors' quantum noise. Above 10\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}Hz, quantum effects are one of the limiting sources of noise. Below 10\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}Hz, classical noise sources, e.g.\nobreakspace  {}seismic motion, limit the sensitivity of the detectors. Above 100\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}Hz, shot noise limits the sensitivity of detectors. Shot noise rises with $f$ due to the arm cavities. Below 100\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}Hz, the quantum noise goes as $1/f$ due to radiation pressure. Coating thermal noise will limit the A+ detectors' sensitivity in a band around $100\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}\si {\hertz }$.\relax }}{13}{figure.caption.10}}
\newlabel{fig:detectorchapterdesignsensitivitycurves}{{1.3}{13}{The light blue and light red lines show the design sensitivity of the aLIGO and LIGO A+ detectors. The noise due to radiation pressure and photon shot noise is shown in dark blue (aLIGO) and dark red (A+). These two noise sources make up the detectors' quantum noise. Above 10\,Hz, quantum effects are one of the limiting sources of noise. Below 10\,Hz, classical noise sources, e.g.~seismic motion, limit the sensitivity of the detectors. Above 100\,Hz, shot noise limits the sensitivity of detectors. Shot noise rises with $f$ due to the arm cavities. Below 100\,Hz, the quantum noise goes as $1/f$ due to radiation pressure. Coating thermal noise will limit the A+ detectors' sensitivity in a band around $100\,\si {\hertz }$.\relax }{figure.caption.10}{}}
