\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.2}{\ignorespaces This sketch shows all the optics in the main beam path as well as the pick-off points for the diagnostic beams for the prototype of the optical layout for the \gls {psl}. After the first amplifier, the power of the laser is $\sim \tmspace  -\thinmuskip {.1667em}60\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}\si {\watt }$, and after the second amplifier, it is $\sim \tmspace  -\thinmuskip {.1667em}120\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}\si {\watt }$. While the laser is being amplitude stabilised, the throughput power of the PMC was $\sim \tmspace  -\thinmuskip {.1667em}100\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}\si {\watt }$. The photodiodes for stabilising the laser's intensity are called ISS PDA and PDB. Within the \gls {dbb} (grey box in the bottom left), there are several photodiodes for characterising beams.\relax }}{37}{figure.caption.13}}
\newlabel{fig:ligo_layout_chapter_layout}{{2.2}{37}{This sketch shows all the optics in the main beam path as well as the pick-off points for the diagnostic beams for the prototype of the optical layout for the \gls {psl}. After the first amplifier, the power of the laser is $\sim \!60\,\si {\watt }$, and after the second amplifier, it is $\sim \!120\,\si {\watt }$. While the laser is being amplitude stabilised, the throughput power of the PMC was $\sim \!100\,\si {\watt }$. The photodiodes for stabilising the laser's intensity are called ISS PDA and PDB. Within the \gls {dbb} (grey box in the bottom left), there are several photodiodes for characterising beams.\relax }{figure.caption.13}{}}
\citation{Kwee2007,kwee_thesis}
\citation{PSL_requirements}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.3}{\ignorespaces Photograph of the \gls {psl} prototype showing the beam path (red lines) from the \gls {npro} (top right) to the second amplifier (bottom right). The beam path up to the first amplifier includes optics for setting the polarisation of the beam, an \gls {eom}, a Faraday isolator and lenses for mode matching. After the first amplifier, the beam passes through a high-power attenuator, mode matching lenses and a Faraday isolator. The first amplifier (bottom left) is monitored using a low-power pick-off beam (centre right).\relax }}{40}{figure.caption.14}}
\newlabel{fig:o191520}{{2.3}{40}{Photograph of the \gls {psl} prototype showing the beam path (red lines) from the \gls {npro} (top right) to the second amplifier (bottom right). The beam path up to the first amplifier includes optics for setting the polarisation of the beam, an \gls {eom}, a Faraday isolator and lenses for mode matching. After the first amplifier, the beam passes through a high-power attenuator, mode matching lenses and a Faraday isolator. The first amplifier (bottom left) is monitored using a low-power pick-off beam (centre right).\relax }{figure.caption.14}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.4}{\ignorespaces Photograph of the layout showing the beam path (red lines) from the second amplifier (bottom left) to the input of the \gls {pmc} (centre right). This beam path includes a high-power attenuator, an \gls {aom}, mode matching lenses for the \gls {pmc} and a thin film polariser. The \gls {pmc} can be seen more clearly in Figure\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {fig:o191531}. The path taken by light reflected from the \gls {pmc} is shown. This light is used to lock the \gls {pmc}. A low-power beam for monitoring the second amplifier is obtained using the transmission through a steering mirror (centre top) for the alignment to the \gls {pmc}.\relax }}{41}{figure.caption.15}}
\newlabel{fig:o191525}{{2.4}{41}{Photograph of the layout showing the beam path (red lines) from the second amplifier (bottom left) to the input of the \gls {pmc} (centre right). This beam path includes a high-power attenuator, an \gls {aom}, mode matching lenses for the \gls {pmc} and a thin film polariser. The \gls {pmc} can be seen more clearly in Figure~\ref {fig:o191531}. The path taken by light reflected from the \gls {pmc} is shown. This light is used to lock the \gls {pmc}. A low-power beam for monitoring the second amplifier is obtained using the transmission through a steering mirror (centre top) for the alignment to the \gls {pmc}.\relax }{figure.caption.15}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.5}{\ignorespaces Photograph of the \gls {pmc} (centre) and the inner \gls {iss} photodiode box (top right). The beam (shown in red) from the second amplifier enters the \gls {pmc}. The majority of this light is sent to the interferometer. A small fraction of the light in transmission of the \gls {pmc} is used to stabilise the intensity and frequency of the laser. These beams are labelled as the \gls {iss} beam and the \gls {ttfss} beam. The \gls {iss} beam is directed into a light tight enclosure. This enclosure contains two photodiodes which are for measuring the intensity noise of the laser. One of these is an in-loop sensor; the other is an out-of-loop sensor. A \gls {qpd} can be used to monitor the alignment of the \gls {iss} photodiodes. The reflected beam from the \gls {pmc} is made up of the junk light in the beam from the second amplifier.\relax }}{42}{figure.caption.16}}
\newlabel{fig:o191531}{{2.5}{42}{Photograph of the \gls {pmc} (centre) and the inner \gls {iss} photodiode box (top right). The beam (shown in red) from the second amplifier enters the \gls {pmc}. The majority of this light is sent to the interferometer. A small fraction of the light in transmission of the \gls {pmc} is used to stabilise the intensity and frequency of the laser. These beams are labelled as the \gls {iss} beam and the \gls {ttfss} beam. The \gls {iss} beam is directed into a light tight enclosure. This enclosure contains two photodiodes which are for measuring the intensity noise of the laser. One of these is an in-loop sensor; the other is an out-of-loop sensor. A \gls {qpd} can be used to monitor the alignment of the \gls {iss} photodiodes. The reflected beam from the \gls {pmc} is made up of the junk light in the beam from the second amplifier.\relax }{figure.caption.16}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.6}{\ignorespaces Photograph of the \gls {dbb} with the beam path highlighted in red. Low power beams are used to measure properties of the laser produced by both amplifiers. The shutters control which amplifier will be characterised. The \gls {dbb} contains motorised lenses and PZT mirrors so that remote mode matching and automatic alignment can be performed. The intensity noise of the beam can be measured with the \gls {rin} photodiode. The \gls {dbb} locking photodiode is used to lock the cavity to the beam. This photodiode is also used during mode scans to measure the higher order content of the beam. \glspl {qpd} separated by 90\si {\degree } of Gouy phase are used to measure the pointing noise of the incoming beam.\relax }}{43}{figure.caption.17}}
\newlabel{fig:o191542}{{2.6}{43}{Photograph of the \gls {dbb} with the beam path highlighted in red. Low power beams are used to measure properties of the laser produced by both amplifiers. The shutters control which amplifier will be characterised. The \gls {dbb} contains motorised lenses and PZT mirrors so that remote mode matching and automatic alignment can be performed. The intensity noise of the beam can be measured with the \gls {rin} photodiode. The \gls {dbb} locking photodiode is used to lock the cavity to the beam. This photodiode is also used during mode scans to measure the higher order content of the beam. \glspl {qpd} separated by 90\si {\degree } of Gouy phase are used to measure the pointing noise of the incoming beam.\relax }{figure.caption.17}{}}
