\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.8}{\ignorespaces Cartoons of the water cooling layout for the prototype laser. Panel (a): The laser will be run with the amplifier heads cooled in series with each other to reduce pointing noise. Panel (b): To perform the amplifier head temperature experiment, the water cooling was arranged so that the amplifiers' head temperatures could be independently controlled.\relax }}{48}{figure.caption.19}}
\newlabel{fig:ligolaserchapterwatercoolingschematic}{{2.8}{48}{Cartoons of the water cooling layout for the prototype laser. Panel (a): The laser will be run with the amplifier heads cooled in series with each other to reduce pointing noise. Panel (b): To perform the amplifier head temperature experiment, the water cooling was arranged so that the amplifiers' head temperatures could be independently controlled.\relax }{figure.caption.19}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.9}{\ignorespaces The top panel: as the second amplifier head's temperature increased, the power produced by it decreased. The bottom panel: the higher order mode content increased as the temperature of the amplifier increased. This means that the power in the fundamental mode decreased as the temperature increased due to two effects: less overall power and worse beam quality.\relax }}{49}{figure.caption.20}}
\newlabel{fig:ligo_laser_chapter_cooling_pwoer_hom}{{2.9}{49}{The top panel: as the second amplifier head's temperature increased, the power produced by it decreased. The bottom panel: the higher order mode content increased as the temperature of the amplifier increased. This means that the power in the fundamental mode decreased as the temperature increased due to two effects: less overall power and worse beam quality.\relax }{figure.caption.20}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.10}{\ignorespaces Mode scans were performed with the \gls {dbb} for each amplifier head temperature. The \gls {dbb}'s auto-alignment system minimised the power in the first order mode. The second order mode for this cavity is located at $\sim \tmspace  -\thinmuskip {.1667em}0.3$ \gls {fsr}; its exact location drifts due to thermal expansion of the cavity. Several percent of the beam's power is in the second and fourth order modes. As the temperature of the amplifier head changes, the second order mode content of the beam changes at the percent level. This second order mode height is shown in the inset. The second order mode changes due to the beam's astigmatism changing.\relax }}{50}{figure.caption.21}}
\newlabel{fig:ligolaserchaptermodescans}{{2.10}{50}{Mode scans were performed with the \gls {dbb} for each amplifier head temperature. The \gls {dbb}'s auto-alignment system minimised the power in the first order mode. The second order mode for this cavity is located at $\sim \!0.3$ \gls {fsr}; its exact location drifts due to thermal expansion of the cavity. Several percent of the beam's power is in the second and fourth order modes. As the temperature of the amplifier head changes, the second order mode content of the beam changes at the percent level. This second order mode height is shown in the inset. The second order mode changes due to the beam's astigmatism changing.\relax }{figure.caption.21}{}}
