\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.14}{\ignorespaces A sketch of how the \gls {aom} transfer function was measured. The components of the intensity stabilisation servo have also been shown. The \gls {pmc} was locked and a signal analyser was used to perform a swept sine measurement. An \gls {ic} (AD734) that performs a square root operation to an input signal was used to linearise the \gls {aom}'s effect on beam power to an input signal. The transfer function was measured between the input to the lineariser and the photodiode used for the intensity stabilisation. The servo electronics are found in\nobreakspace  {}\cite  {ISS_inner_loop_rack} and\nobreakspace  {}\cite  {ISS_inner_loop_photodiode}.\relax }}{61}{figure.caption.25}}
\newlabel{fig:ligolaserchapteraomtfmeasurement}{{2.14}{61}{A sketch of how the \gls {aom} transfer function was measured. The components of the intensity stabilisation servo have also been shown. The \gls {pmc} was locked and a signal analyser was used to perform a swept sine measurement. An \gls {ic} (AD734) that performs a square root operation to an input signal was used to linearise the \gls {aom}'s effect on beam power to an input signal. The transfer function was measured between the input to the lineariser and the photodiode used for the intensity stabilisation. The servo electronics are found in~\cite {ISS_inner_loop_rack} and~\cite {ISS_inner_loop_photodiode}.\relax }{figure.caption.25}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.15}{\ignorespaces Using the layout shown in Figure\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {fig:ligolaserchapteraomtfmeasurement}, the transfer function between the input to the linearisation stage before the \gls {aom} to the light power change on the \gls {iss} photodiode was measured. The response was normalised by dividing by the DC power on the photodiode. The transfer function is flat over the region where the open-loop gain needs to be greater than one ($\sim \tmspace  -\thinmuskip {.1667em} 10\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}\si {\kilo \hertz }$). There is some phase lag between 10--100\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}kHz; this is where the unity gain point of the servo will be and so this needs to be accounted for when determining if the loop will be stable. The corner frequency of the \gls {pmc} is around $\sim \tmspace  -\thinmuskip {.1667em}1\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}\rm  {MHz}$, so this did not affect the measurement.\relax }}{62}{figure.caption.26}}
\newlabel{fig:ligolaserchapteraomtransferfunction}{{2.15}{62}{Using the layout shown in Figure~\ref {fig:ligolaserchapteraomtfmeasurement}, the transfer function between the input to the linearisation stage before the \gls {aom} to the light power change on the \gls {iss} photodiode was measured. The response was normalised by dividing by the DC power on the photodiode. The transfer function is flat over the region where the open-loop gain needs to be greater than one ($\sim \! 10\,\si {\kilo \hertz }$). There is some phase lag between 10--100\,kHz; this is where the unity gain point of the servo will be and so this needs to be accounted for when determining if the loop will be stable. The corner frequency of the \gls {pmc} is around $\sim \!1\,\rm {MHz}$, so this did not affect the measurement.\relax }{figure.caption.26}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.16}{\ignorespaces A sketch of how the current shunt transfer function was measured. The \gls {pmc} was locked and a signal analyser was used to perform a swept sine measurement. The current of one of the pump-diodes in the second amplifier was modulated by applying a signal to a current shunt. The transfer function was measured between the input to the current shunt electronics and the photodiode used for the intensity stabilisation.\relax }}{64}{figure.caption.27}}
\newlabel{fig:ligolaserchaptercstfmeasurement}{{2.16}{64}{A sketch of how the current shunt transfer function was measured. The \gls {pmc} was locked and a signal analyser was used to perform a swept sine measurement. The current of one of the pump-diodes in the second amplifier was modulated by applying a signal to a current shunt. The transfer function was measured between the input to the current shunt electronics and the photodiode used for the intensity stabilisation.\relax }{figure.caption.27}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.17}{\ignorespaces Using the layout shown in Figure\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {fig:ligolaserchaptercstfmeasurement}, the transfer function between the current shunt input electronics and the intensity stabilisation photodiode was measured. The transfer function was normalised by dividing by the DC power on the photodiode. At 10\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}Hz, where the noise requirement is hardest to meet, the gain of the transfer function changes significantly depending on the pump-diode's DC current. The phase at this frequency does not matter as it is not near the unity gain point of the servo.\relax }}{65}{figure.caption.28}}
\newlabel{fig:ligolaserchaptercurrentshunttf}{{2.17}{65}{Using the layout shown in Figure~\ref {fig:ligolaserchaptercstfmeasurement}, the transfer function between the current shunt input electronics and the intensity stabilisation photodiode was measured. The transfer function was normalised by dividing by the DC power on the photodiode. At 10\,Hz, where the noise requirement is hardest to meet, the gain of the transfer function changes significantly depending on the pump-diode's DC current. The phase at this frequency does not matter as it is not near the unity gain point of the servo.\relax }{figure.caption.28}{}}
