\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {2.7.2}Frequency Noise Measurement}{84}{subsection.2.7.2}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.27}{\ignorespaces The open-loop transfer function was measured using the layout shown in Figure\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {fig:ligolaserchapterfrequencynoisemeasurementsketch}. This is shown in red. The transfer function of the electronics without the `boost' provided by an extra integrator stage was measured and is shown in blue. The model of this is shown in orange. The boosted electronics model is shown in purple. The green line shows the expected servo gain based on the boosted electronics model and the measurements shown in Figure\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {fig:ligolaserchaptermixercalibration} and Figure\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {fig:ligolaserchapterpmcscan}. This model is within 2\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}dB of the measurement above the unity gain point. The PZT inducing unintended motion in the cavity as it goes through resonance may explain the phase a gain discrepancy between the model and the measurement below the unity gain point. The unity gain point was measured to be at $\sim \tmspace  -\thinmuskip {.1667em} 800$\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}Hz.\relax }}{85}{figure.caption.39}}
\newlabel{fig:ligolaserchapterpmcloopmodeltf}{{2.27}{85}{The open-loop transfer function was measured using the layout shown in Figure~\ref {fig:ligolaserchapterfrequencynoisemeasurementsketch}. This is shown in red. The transfer function of the electronics without the `boost' provided by an extra integrator stage was measured and is shown in blue. The model of this is shown in orange. The boosted electronics model is shown in purple. The green line shows the expected servo gain based on the boosted electronics model and the measurements shown in Figure~\ref {fig:ligolaserchaptermixercalibration} and Figure~\ref {fig:ligolaserchapterpmcscan}. This model is within 2\,dB of the measurement above the unity gain point. The PZT inducing unintended motion in the cavity as it goes through resonance may explain the phase a gain discrepancy between the model and the measurement below the unity gain point. The unity gain point was measured to be at $\sim \! 800$\,Hz.\relax }{figure.caption.39}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {2.8}Conclusion}{86}{section.2.8}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.28}{\ignorespaces The mixer signal combined with the open-loop transfer function is shown in red. This is equivalent to the frequency noise of the system. The PZT signal should agree with this measurement below the unity gain point ($\sim \tmspace  -\thinmuskip {.1667em} 800$\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}Hz). A signal was applied to the PZT at 1111\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}Hz. This is below the corner frequency of the PZT and above the unity gain point for the servo. This should match with the mixer signal. There is a factor of two between these two measurements. This can be attributed to imperfect calibration due to nominal parameters of the \gls {pmc} being used. The typical noise expected from an \gls {npro} is shown by the dashed black line. Between 100\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}Hz and 1000\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}Hz, there is a bulge in the frequency noise. This is likely due to acoustically driven noise of the \gls {pmc} as the \gls {pmc} is not vacuum sealed. This can be converted to length noise with a factor of $\sim \tmspace  -\thinmuskip {.1667em}10^{-14}\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}\rm  {m/Hz}$.\relax }}{87}{figure.caption.40}}
\newlabel{fig:ligolaserchapterfrequencynoise}{{2.28}{87}{The mixer signal combined with the open-loop transfer function is shown in red. This is equivalent to the frequency noise of the system. The PZT signal should agree with this measurement below the unity gain point ($\sim \! 800$\,Hz). A signal was applied to the PZT at 1111\,Hz. This is below the corner frequency of the PZT and above the unity gain point for the servo. This should match with the mixer signal. There is a factor of two between these two measurements. This can be attributed to imperfect calibration due to nominal parameters of the \gls {pmc} being used. The typical noise expected from an \gls {npro} is shown by the dashed black line. Between 100\,Hz and 1000\,Hz, there is a bulge in the frequency noise. This is likely due to acoustically driven noise of the \gls {pmc} as the \gls {pmc} is not vacuum sealed. This can be converted to length noise with a factor of $\sim \!10^{-14}\,\rm {m/Hz}$.\relax }{figure.caption.40}{}}
