\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.1}{\ignorespaces Sketch showing the parts of the aLIGO detectors discussed in this chapter. A sketch showing more of the detector is shown in Figure\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {fig:ligolayout}. The angles and lengths are exaggerated for clarity. Light from the arm cavities is incident upon the beam splitter (BS). As BS is wedged, the light going towards the \gls {src} comes out an angle. Differential arm length signals encoded onto this light are directed towards SR3, SR2, and then the \gls {srm}. Between the \gls {srm} and the \gls {omc}, there are optics for steering and mode matching (OM1-3). Finally, to strip away junk light, the light enters the \gls {omc}. The light emerging from the \gls {omc} is detected by photodiodes.\relax }}{92}{figure.caption.41}}
\newlabel{fig:beamparameterchapterligolayout}{{3.1}{92}{Sketch showing the parts of the aLIGO detectors discussed in this chapter. A sketch showing more of the detector is shown in Figure~\ref {fig:ligolayout}. The angles and lengths are exaggerated for clarity. Light from the arm cavities is incident upon the beam splitter (BS). As BS is wedged, the light going towards the \gls {src} comes out an angle. Differential arm length signals encoded onto this light are directed towards SR3, SR2, and then the \gls {srm}. Between the \gls {srm} and the \gls {omc}, there are optics for steering and mode matching (OM1-3). Finally, to strip away junk light, the light enters the \gls {omc}. The light emerging from the \gls {omc} is detected by photodiodes.\relax }{figure.caption.41}{}}
