\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.2}{\ignorespaces The simulation of a Michelson interferometer with a thin beam splitter is shown in blue. The same simulation was done with a thick beam splitter and this is shown in orange. The analytic behaviour of a Michelson interferometer (Equation\nobreakspace  {}(\ref  {eq:michelson})) is shown by the dashed black line. The tuning of one of the arm mirrors was changed and the power at the \gls {as} port was simulated. Comparing the outputs of the simulation to Equation\nobreakspace  {}(\ref  {eq:michelson}) shows that thick beam splitters should be used.\relax }}{102}{figure.caption.43}}
\newlabel{fig:thinvsthick}{{3.2}{102}{The simulation of a Michelson interferometer with a thin beam splitter is shown in blue. The same simulation was done with a thick beam splitter and this is shown in orange. The analytic behaviour of a Michelson interferometer (Equation~(\ref {eq:michelson})) is shown by the dashed black line. The tuning of one of the arm mirrors was changed and the power at the \gls {as} port was simulated. Comparing the outputs of the simulation to Equation~(\ref {eq:michelson}) shows that thick beam splitters should be used.\relax }{figure.caption.43}{}}
