\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {6.5.1}The Dependence of Dark Noise and Dark Current on Reverse Bias in Extended InGaAs Photodiodes}{189}{subsection.6.5.1}}
\newlabel{sec:pd_chapter_room_temp_experiment}{{6.5.1}{189}{The Dependence of Dark Noise and Dark Current on Reverse Bias in Extended InGaAs Photodiodes}{subsection.6.5.1}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.5}{\ignorespaces The dark noise of the FD10D increases as its reverse bias is increased. Above 0.6\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}V, the dark noise of the FD10D would limit the noise of a 10\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}mA current above 10\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}Hz. As the noise spectral densities have no knees, the function $A/f^{\beta }$ can be fitted to the data. See Appendix\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {sec:pd_appendix_bias_dark_noise} for the dark noise spectra of other photodiodes. Below 0.9\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}V reverse bias, the spectra fall as $f^{-0.45}$ and above 0.9\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}V bias the spectra fall as $f^{-0.4}$. The values for $\beta $ are typical for `$1/f$' noise\nobreakspace  {}\cite  {Hooge1981}. The dark noise at 10\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}Hz for each reverse bias is shown in Figure\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {fig:pdchapterdarkcurrentmegaplot} alongside the results for the other photodiodes that were tested.\relax }}{191}{figure.caption.79}}
\newlabel{fig:pdchapterfd10ddarknoise}{{6.5}{191}{The dark noise of the FD10D increases as its reverse bias is increased. Above 0.6\,V, the dark noise of the FD10D would limit the noise of a 10\,mA current above 10\,Hz. As the noise spectral densities have no knees, the function $A/f^{\beta }$ can be fitted to the data. See Appendix~\ref {sec:pd_appendix_bias_dark_noise} for the dark noise spectra of other photodiodes. Below 0.9\,V reverse bias, the spectra fall as $f^{-0.45}$ and above 0.9\,V bias the spectra fall as $f^{-0.4}$. The values for $\beta $ are typical for `$1/f$' noise~\cite {Hooge1981}. The dark noise at 10\,Hz for each reverse bias is shown in Figure~\ref {fig:pdchapterdarkcurrentmegaplot} alongside the results for the other photodiodes that were tested.\relax }{figure.caption.79}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.6}{\ignorespaces Left panel: the dark current for each photodiode was measured. Photodiodes of a similar cut-off wavelength share the same marker shape. The 1\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}mm photodiodes have larger markers than the 0.5\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}mm photodiodes. See Table\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {tab:pd_chapter_photodiodes_tested} for information about the size and cut-off wavelength for each photodiode. Right panel: the dark noise at 10\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}Hz for each photodiode. The dashed line indicates the shot noise for a 10\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}mA current. The data used to create these plots are shown in Appendix\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {app:dark_noise_rev_bias_plots}.\relax }}{192}{figure.caption.80}}
\newlabel{fig:pdchapterdarkcurrentmegaplot}{{6.6}{192}{Left panel: the dark current for each photodiode was measured. Photodiodes of a similar cut-off wavelength share the same marker shape. The 1\,mm photodiodes have larger markers than the 0.5\,mm photodiodes. See Table~\ref {tab:pd_chapter_photodiodes_tested} for information about the size and cut-off wavelength for each photodiode. Right panel: the dark noise at 10\,Hz for each photodiode. The dashed line indicates the shot noise for a 10\,mA current. The data used to create these plots are shown in Appendix~\ref {app:dark_noise_rev_bias_plots}.\relax }{figure.caption.80}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.7}{\ignorespaces The difference in dark noise between two nominally identical photodiodes (g12182-010k) under the same operating conditions (room temperature at 1\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}V bias). Photodiode 1 performs nearly 100 times better than photodiode 2. For the duration of this experiment, the photodiodes were handled with suitable \gls {esd} precautions. This difference in noise may be explained by differing numbers of defects or by \gls {esd} damage prior to purchase.\relax }}{193}{figure.caption.81}}
\newlabel{fig:pdchapterg12182-010knoisecompare}{{6.7}{193}{The difference in dark noise between two nominally identical photodiodes (g12182-010k) under the same operating conditions (room temperature at 1\,V bias). Photodiode 1 performs nearly 100 times better than photodiode 2. For the duration of this experiment, the photodiodes were handled with suitable \gls {esd} precautions. This difference in noise may be explained by differing numbers of defects or by \gls {esd} damage prior to purchase.\relax }{figure.caption.81}{}}
