\newlabel{fig:mzchapterinairmz}{{7.2}{216}{Photograph of the Mach-Zehnder baseplate. The polarising beam splitters were part of an earlier design; they were removed when the measurements with this apparatus were made. \relax }{figure.caption.92}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.3}{\ignorespaces Sketch of the optical layout. The laser was generated by an \gls {npro}. The power of the laser was monitored using a photodiode immediately after the laser. The beam's power and polarisation were controlled with a half wave plate and polarising beam splitter (PBS). The Mach-Zehnder was mounted on a baseplate, and it was acoustically shielded by a plastic box and rubber feet. The splitting ratio of BS2 was measured to be 50:50. An \gls {nd} filter was used to decrease the power in one of the paths within the Mach-Zehnder. \relax }}{217}{figure.caption.93}}
\newlabel{fig:mzchapterinairsketch}{{7.3}{217}{Sketch of the optical layout. The laser was generated by an \gls {npro}. The power of the laser was monitored using a photodiode immediately after the laser. The beam's power and polarisation were controlled with a half wave plate and polarising beam splitter (PBS). The Mach-Zehnder was mounted on a baseplate, and it was acoustically shielded by a plastic box and rubber feet. The splitting ratio of BS2 was measured to be 50:50. An \gls {nd} filter was used to decrease the power in one of the paths within the Mach-Zehnder. \relax }{figure.caption.93}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.4}{\ignorespaces The transfer function of the electronics for sending, receiving and whitening the photodiode signal was modelled and measured. This gave the desired level of SNR to make shot noise limited measurements above $\sim \tmspace  -\thinmuskip {.1667em}200\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}\rm  {Hz}$.\relax }}{219}{figure.caption.94}}
\newlabel{fig:mzchapterinairsendrecievefilter}{{7.4}{219}{The transfer function of the electronics for sending, receiving and whitening the photodiode signal was modelled and measured. This gave the desired level of SNR to make shot noise limited measurements above $\sim \!200\,\rm {Hz}$.\relax }{figure.caption.94}{}}
