\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.5}{\ignorespaces Circuit diagram for the electronics used to keep the in-air Mach-Zehnder locked to the middle of a fringe. The inputs PD\_A and PD\_B connect to two identical transimpedance amplifiers (see Appendix\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {sec:circuits_appendix_variable_bias_ad797}). The first stage subtracts the signals from the photodiodes and provides a 1\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}Hz roll-off. The second stage allowed for a signal to be applied to the PZT. The final stage boosted the signal to around 100V. The final resistor was used to prevent the OPA454 oscillating when driving the PZT, as the PZT is a capacitive load.\relax }}{221}{figure.caption.95}}
\newlabel{fig:mzinairservocircuit}{{7.5}{221}{Circuit diagram for the electronics used to keep the in-air Mach-Zehnder locked to the middle of a fringe. The inputs PD\_A and PD\_B connect to two identical transimpedance amplifiers (see Appendix~\ref {sec:circuits_appendix_variable_bias_ad797}). The first stage subtracts the signals from the photodiodes and provides a 1\,Hz roll-off. The second stage allowed for a signal to be applied to the PZT. The final stage boosted the signal to around 100V. The final resistor was used to prevent the OPA454 oscillating when driving the PZT, as the PZT is a capacitive load.\relax }{figure.caption.95}{}}
