\newlabel{sec:mz_chapter_shot_noise_measurement}{{7.1.3}{222}{Measurement of Shot Noise}{subsection.7.1.3}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.6}{\ignorespaces There are two ways of calibrating the differential photodiode signal that will be generated by a change in optical path length between the two paths in the Mach-Zehnder. Method (a) is to measure a ramp signal that is applied to the PZT while simultaneously measuring the photodiode signals. The measurement of this is shown by the blue crosses, and the fit to this data is shown by the blue line. Method (b) is to measure the power in the local oscillator and signal beams when they are not interfering. From this, Equation\nobreakspace  {}(\ref  {eq:bhdforligo_interference_at_a_beam_splitter}) can be used to find the response of the Mach-Zehnder. This is shown by the red line. Thus, the blue and red data are independent of each other. The two calibrations agree to within 10\%. The gradient of $V_{\rm  {BHD}}$ is used to get the conversion factor for a change in beam power to a change in differential path length.\relax }}{223}{figure.caption.96}}
\newlabel{fig:mzchapterinaircalibration}{{7.6}{223}{There are two ways of calibrating the differential photodiode signal that will be generated by a change in optical path length between the two paths in the Mach-Zehnder. Method (a) is to measure a ramp signal that is applied to the PZT while simultaneously measuring the photodiode signals. The measurement of this is shown by the blue crosses, and the fit to this data is shown by the blue line. Method (b) is to measure the power in the local oscillator and signal beams when they are not interfering. From this, Equation~(\ref {eq:bhdforligo_interference_at_a_beam_splitter}) can be used to find the response of the Mach-Zehnder. This is shown by the red line. Thus, the blue and red data are independent of each other. The two calibrations agree to within 10\%. The gradient of $V_{\rm {BHD}}$ is used to get the conversion factor for a change in beam power to a change in differential path length.\relax }{figure.caption.96}{}}
\citation{Chua2014}
\citation{Graef2014}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.7}{\ignorespaces The amplitude spectral density of the difference between the two photodiode's signals ($\rm  {S_{BHD}}$) is shown in blue. The peak at 6033\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}Hz was injected to demonstrate this measurement corresponds to differential arm length modulations. The expected shot noise is based on Equation\nobreakspace  {}(\ref  {eq:MZ_chapter_shot_noise_level}), and this is shown by the dashed red line. The noise measured at frequencies above 5500\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}Hz is within 5\% of the expected shot noise. Below 5500\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}Hz, acoustic noise limited the Mach-Zehnder's sensitivity. This plot is for emphasising the shot noise limit of the measurement in metres; for an analysis of the noise due to scattered light, see Figure\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {fig:mzchapterfullnoisespectrumwithcouplings}.\relax }}{225}{figure.caption.97}}
\newlabel{fig:mzchapterfullnoisespectrum}{{7.7}{225}{The amplitude spectral density of the difference between the two photodiode's signals ($\rm {S_{BHD}}$) is shown in blue. The peak at 6033\,Hz was injected to demonstrate this measurement corresponds to differential arm length modulations. The expected shot noise is based on Equation~(\ref {eq:MZ_chapter_shot_noise_level}), and this is shown by the dashed red line. The noise measured at frequencies above 5500\,Hz is within 5\% of the expected shot noise. Below 5500\,Hz, acoustic noise limited the Mach-Zehnder's sensitivity. This plot is for emphasising the shot noise limit of the measurement in metres; for an analysis of the noise due to scattered light, see Figure~\ref {fig:mzchapterfullnoisespectrumwithcouplings}.\relax }{figure.caption.97}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.8}{\ignorespaces The amplitude spectral density of the difference between the two photodiodes' signals ($\rm  {S_{BHD}}$) is shown in blue. The frequency noise that couples via a difference in path lengths between the two arms was estimated, and this is shown in green. The estimated spectrum due to 300\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}fW of scattered light is shown in yellow.\relax }}{226}{figure.caption.98}}
\newlabel{fig:mzchapterfullnoisespectrumwithcouplings}{{7.8}{226}{The amplitude spectral density of the difference between the two photodiodes' signals ($\rm {S_{BHD}}$) is shown in blue. The frequency noise that couples via a difference in path lengths between the two arms was estimated, and this is shown in green. The estimated spectrum due to 300\,fW of scattered light is shown in yellow.\relax }{figure.caption.98}{}}
