\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.11}{\ignorespaces Flow diagram showing each block of the servo used to lock the in-vacuum Mach-Zehnder. The digital part of the servo is shown in the dashed area. The injection point A and measurement point B were used to calibrate the servo (see Figure\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {fig:mzchapterservotransferfunction}) are around a times one block. The mirrors motion that the servo must correct for is represeted by the signal $n_M$. The information about the Mach-Zehnder differential arm length is encoded by two signals that are 180\si {\degree } out of phase. The whitening and dewhitening filters are paler as these do not affect the gain of the loop.\relax }}{233}{figure.caption.102}}
\newlabel{fig:mzchapterflowdiagraminvacuumservo}{{7.11}{233}{Flow diagram showing each block of the servo used to lock the in-vacuum Mach-Zehnder. The digital part of the servo is shown in the dashed area. The injection point A and measurement point B were used to calibrate the servo (see Figure~\ref {fig:mzchapterservotransferfunction}) are around a times one block. The mirrors motion that the servo must correct for is represeted by the signal $n_M$. The information about the Mach-Zehnder differential arm length is encoded by two signals that are 180\si {\degree } out of phase. The whitening and dewhitening filters are paler as these do not affect the gain of the loop.\relax }{figure.caption.102}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.12}{\ignorespaces Panel (a) and panel (b) show the servo's open and closed-loop transfer function. This was calibrated using the factors from \ref  {tab:MZ_chapter_servo_model}. Panel (c) shows a measurement where a signal was injected at point A in the servo and measured at point B (see Figure\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {fig:mzchapterflowdiagraminvacuumservo}); these two points are separated by a unity gain block, and so the ratio of these signals represents the closed-loop transfer function of the servo. These measurements are in agreement with the model to within 0.1\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}dB.\relax }}{234}{figure.caption.103}}
\newlabel{fig:mzchapterservotransferfunction}{{7.12}{234}{Panel (a) and panel (b) show the servo's open and closed-loop transfer function. This was calibrated using the factors from \ref {tab:MZ_chapter_servo_model}. Panel (c) shows a measurement where a signal was injected at point A in the servo and measured at point B (see Figure~\ref {fig:mzchapterflowdiagraminvacuumservo}); these two points are separated by a unity gain block, and so the ratio of these signals represents the closed-loop transfer function of the servo. These measurements are in agreement with the model to within 0.1\,dB.\relax }{figure.caption.103}{}}
\citation{Meers1991a}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {7.2.4}The Effect of Misalignment on the Size of a Signal in an Interferometer}{235}{subsection.7.2.4}}
\newlabel{eq:MZ_chapter_misalignment}{{7.2}{235}{The Effect of Misalignment on the Size of a Signal in an Interferometer}{equation.7.2.2}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.13}{\ignorespaces Scattering caused there to be a high level of noise in the differential arm length signal. Many peaks related to the balanced homodyne detector suspension can be seen around 30\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}Hz, and this motion generated phase modulations in the scattered light. The noise was caused by the interference between the scattered light and the signal-carrying light on the photodiodes. The power of the scattered light was estimated to be $\sim \tmspace  -\thinmuskip {.1667em}20\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}\si {\micro \watt }$. For this measurement, shot noise would have been at $\sim \tmspace  -\thinmuskip {.1667em} 10^{-15}\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}\rm  {m}/\sqrt  {\rm  {Hz}}$.\relax }}{236}{figure.caption.104}}
\newlabel{fig:mzchaptersuspendeddiffarm}{{7.13}{236}{Scattering caused there to be a high level of noise in the differential arm length signal. Many peaks related to the balanced homodyne detector suspension can be seen around 30\,Hz, and this motion generated phase modulations in the scattered light. The noise was caused by the interference between the scattered light and the signal-carrying light on the photodiodes. The power of the scattered light was estimated to be $\sim \!20\,\si {\micro \watt }$. For this measurement, shot noise would have been at $\sim \! 10^{-15}\,\rm {m}/\sqrt {\rm {Hz}}$.\relax }{figure.caption.104}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {7.3}Conclusion}{237}{section.7.3}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.14}{\ignorespaces Top panel: As the Mach-Zehnder was misaligned, the signal due to the differential arm length modulation decreased. The blue points show the measured signal as a function of misalignment. The data was fit to Equation\nobreakspace  {}(\ref  {eq:MZ_chapter_misalignment}), and this is shown in red. Bottom panel: the weighted residuals of the measurements to the fit show no pattern, indicating that the model used was appropriate.\relax }}{238}{figure.caption.105}}
\newlabel{fig:mzchaptermisalignmentplot}{{7.14}{238}{Top panel: As the Mach-Zehnder was misaligned, the signal due to the differential arm length modulation decreased. The blue points show the measured signal as a function of misalignment. The data was fit to Equation~(\ref {eq:MZ_chapter_misalignment}), and this is shown in red. Bottom panel: the weighted residuals of the measurements to the fit show no pattern, indicating that the model used was appropriate.\relax }{figure.caption.105}{}}
