\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {C.1}{\ignorespaces This MEDM interface is used by operators of the interferometer to control and monitor the status of the \gls {pmc}. Several inputs and outputs can be monitored simultaneously, and locking the \gls {pmc} is reduced to simply pressing a button (\texttt  {LOCK}). Most aspects of the locking servo can be adjusted with \gls {cds}, e.g. the \texttt  {gain} slider adjusts the gain of the servo, thus operators have a large amount of freedom when they are trying to optimise the \gls {pmc}'s locking capabilities.\relax }}{272}{figure.caption.130}}
\newlabel{fig:medmscreen}{{C.1}{272}{This MEDM interface is used by operators of the interferometer to control and monitor the status of the \gls {pmc}. Several inputs and outputs can be monitored simultaneously, and locking the \gls {pmc} is reduced to simply pressing a button (\texttt {LOCK}). Most aspects of the locking servo can be adjusted with \gls {cds}, e.g. the \texttt {gain} slider adjusts the gain of the servo, thus operators have a large amount of freedom when they are trying to optimise the \gls {pmc}'s locking capabilities.\relax }{figure.caption.130}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {C.2}{\ignorespaces Noise floor of the digitised input signals recorded by the Glasgow \gls {cds}. This was converted into a voltage using $2^{15}$ counts per 10 Volts. The ripples above 1\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}kHz arise due to the 3rd order low pass filter at 9\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}kHz. Below $\sim 10$\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}Hz, the input noise rises as $1/f$.\relax }}{273}{figure.caption.131}}
\newlabel{fig:mzchaptercdsnoise}{{C.2}{273}{Noise floor of the digitised input signals recorded by the Glasgow \gls {cds}. This was converted into a voltage using $2^{15}$ counts per 10 Volts. The ripples above 1\,kHz arise due to the 3rd order low pass filter at 9\,kHz. Below $\sim 10$\,Hz, the input noise rises as $1/f$.\relax }{figure.caption.131}{}}
